+      # SCOPE. It ran `git revert` on the whole merge commit, so a converge
+      # failing because a binary was missing from a runner would have removed
+      # 83 files - the attestation gate, the push guard, the mutation ledger,
+      # the git hooks and eleven building-code assertions among them. Only
+      # `management/` drives the estate; nothing else here is applied by a
+      # converge, and nothing else should move because one failed. The estate
+      # rule is to enumerate what will be acted on by address, so a targeted
+      # action cannot become unbounded, and a whole-merge revert is the
+      # opposite construction.
+      #
+      # SIGNATURE. It used plain git, which produces unsigned commits, and
+      # `required_signatures` applies to every branch here - so this path could
+      # only ever fail, and its first real execution was its first test. The
+      # commit is built through the Git Data API instead: GITHUB_TOKEN is an
+      # installation token, and a commit created with one comes back signed
+      # with GitHub's own key. No new credential.
+      #
+      # REOPENING. GitHub cannot reopen a merged pull request, so the thing
+      # asked for is not available. The revert pull request carries the link
+      # instead, and reverting it restores the change - which is the same
+      # outcome by the only route that exists.
+      - name: Revert management/, as a signed pull request
         if: steps.verdict.outputs.exact == 'true'
